A||N||6||87GTBro||Cold start problem - fuel pressure related||||5||
Z||000000||87GTBro||07-04-2000||12:04 AM||||I have an 86 GT 2.8.  Recently it has become increasingly difficult to start my engine while cold.  The hardest time is first thing in the morning.  It takes a good five minutes of cranking sometimes to get it to start.<BR>I recently checked the fuel pressure and it was reading half of what it should read.  I replaced the Fuel Pressure Regulator, but I am still having the same problem.<BR>When I turn the key switch on, I can hear the fuel pump relay click and the fuel pump whirring for the normal 2 seconds, but for some reason I still don't get enough pressure to start the car.<BR>My question is, would a bad fuel pump relay cause the fuel pump to fail in reaching the proper pressure?  This would definitely be the cheapest solution, but I am not sure it is the problem.<P>Any help would be greatly appreciated.||||reg||5
Z||000001||terryk||07-04-2000||12:30 AM||||Sounds like the pump, the pulsator is leaking or the like.<P>My guess is pump.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||86se/lambo||07-05-2000||11:00 PM||||yep it can definately be the relay. there is another relay next to the fuel pump relay(they look the same) against the firewall.. i<BR>think it is for the oil pressure, but i remember that you can swap the two..if it fires right up you have a bad relay. the way i understand it is if the fuel pump relay is not functioning after much cranking it will bypass and go through the oil sender relay. i'm sure someone else here can attest.. let us know how it works out. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||87GTBro||07-05-2000||11:28 PM||||OK here's the deal.  I changed the relay out but it didnt do a bit of good.  I have not checked my fuel pressure yet since I replaced the fuel pressure regulator.  I guess I should have, I just dont have the time.<BR>If I check the fuel pressure and it is still low, then that means my fuel pump is going out.  If it is fine, then I have a problem with ignition.  Does that sound right?<BR>I will then check the spark and ignition coil and all that.  It might just be my ignition module.  <P>It is so hard to pinpoint the problem!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||86se/lambo||07-06-2000||07:10 PM||||well..yah you definately have to start there.guess that you had checked or adjusted that regulator when you put it in. so did you swap the relays or just go buy one?<BR>well let us know what happens<BR>see ya<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||07-07-2000||06:25 PM||||Assuming the ignition is fine and you aren't flooding the engine (do you smell raw gas?)......<P>When you start the car, the ECM turns on the FP relay for two seconds to pressurize the system. If you hit the key, it will crank for a seconds or two and start. That's normal. <P>If the FP relay is bad, you have to crank the engine until the oil pressure is 4PSI or greater. This switch is in parallel with the FP relay. 4PSI is usually hit within a few seconds. Might be as long as five or ten if the oil is thin.<P>The next item is the cold start injection. It will squirt fuel for 8-16 seconds (8 on the Fiero I think) when the coolant is under 116 degrees F. When the CSI is dead, it's not unusual for the crank time to head towards the 5 second+ range. If the FP relay is *also* bad, the time might get a bit longer at least until the oil pressure hits 4 PSI.<P>Five minutes of cranking is not the relay, the oil pressure switch or even the CSI.<P>You need to check the fuel pressure with a gauge or an A/C manifold set. One hose will fit the fuel rail. Blow the gauge out afterwards.<P>Turn the key on but don't start the car. Does the pressure come up to 30PSI+? If yes, the FP relay is good, if not, the relay is bad or the ECM is bad (not turning on the FP relay).<P>If the pressure didn't come up, crank the engine. Does the pressure come up after a few seconds of cranking (it dang well better if the oil pressure gauge is moving)? If so, the FP relay is bad and the oil pressure switch is taking over. <P>If not, is it possible the fuel regulator is bad, but the total pressure range is not enough to drop it to zero. Still this should be considered, but I doubt it.<P>I would then suspect something in the tank and that is the pump or the pulsator.<P>While 30PSI is not great, it is enough to run the engine and start it in less than 5 minutes. 40PSI+ is fine period. ||||reg||3
Z||000006||gmh||01-13-2001||11:48 PM||||I have a 88 4cy, and have been going thru the same problems as you have, i have replaced everything, fuel pump, regulator injector, ignition module, plugs wires, ect.<BR>Turned out to be dirty and loose connections to the ecm, and also make sure you have a good battery. The problem has drove me crazy for several months. Good luck.||||reg||1
